  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts a conventional high-power coherent optical transmitter with a much more compact integrated alternative. On the left, the traditional system is shown as a chain of separate parts: a semiconductor laser feeds a coherent modulator, the signal is routed through optical fibres into a large high-power fibre amplifier, and a collimation lens then forms the output beam. This arrangement is visually bulky, with multiple interconnected modules needed to generate a coherent high-power beam.\n\nOn the right, the same overall function appears to be condensed into a single coherently modulated photonic-crystal surface-emitting laser, labeled PCSEL. The device directly emits the modulated beam without the external fibre amplifier, fibre links, and separate lens-heavy architecture seen in the conventional setup. A large arrow between the two layouts emphasizes the transition from the multi-component transmitter to the integrated PCSEL-based high-power coherent transmitter.\n\nThe main message is the dramatic reduction in system size and mass: the blue text states that the integrated approach can be “1/1,000 or less in volume and weight.” Overall, the image highlights miniaturization and simplification of coherent high-power laser transmission by replacing a complex fibre-based assembly with a single compact laser device.",
